{"parentUuid":"30d1a74b-bb7d-4097-9cf0-aacbbdf11496","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01FzSypzDqJFvmFQCzAYkRJk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Here's the design — five short sections. Tell me after each whether it looks right.\n\n---\n\n### Section 1 — Lock package layout\n\nA new tiny package `cmd/entire/cli/lockfile/` with three files:\n\n- `lockfile.go` — public API: `func Acquire(path string) (*Lock, error)`, `func (*Lock) Release() error`, `var ErrLocked = errors.New(\"...\")`. The `Lock` struct holds the open `*os.File` and the OS-specific lock state. Acquire opens with `O_CREATE|O_RDWR|O_CLOEXEC`, mode `0600`, calls the OS-specific `tryLockExclusive(fd)` shim, and on success truncates + writes the current PID (advisory metadata). On `ErrLocked`, it reads whatever PID is in the file (if any) so the caller can include it in the error message, then closes the fd.\n- `lockfile_unix.go` (`//go:build unix`) — uses `golang.org/x/sys/unix.Flock(fd, LOCK_EX|LOCK_NB)`. `EWOULDBLOCK` → `ErrLocked`. Release calls `unix.Flock(fd, LOCK_UN)` and closes the file.\n- `lockfile_windows.go` (`//go:build windows`) — uses `golang.org/x/sys/windows.LockFileEx(handle, LOCKFILE_EXCLUSIVE_LOCK|LOCKFILE_FAIL_IMMEDIATELY, 0, MAXDWORD, MAXDWORD, &overlapped)`. `ERROR_LOCK_VIOLATION` → `ErrLocked`. Release calls `UnlockFileEx` and closes the file.\n\nThe lock file is **never deleted** — leaving it on disk avoids a `delete-then-acquire` race on the next run, and stale files are harmless because the OS lock state is what counts. The PID-in-file is purely advisory diagnostic metadata for the error message.\n\nWhy a dedicated package: the CLI has no existing file-lock infrastructure; isolating the build-tag split makes `migrate.go` itself unaware of the platform shim, and gives us one obvious place to write unit tests against.\n\nOK on Section 1?"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usag