erence`) are designed for fast condense-vs-condense\ncontention, not for the long, multi-ref migration pipeline.\n\n## Goal\n\nPrevent two `entire migrate` invocations from running concurrently\nagainst the same git repository.\n\n## Non-goals\n\n- Preventing concurrent `entire migrate` against **different**\n  repositories (no value, would just block dev workflows).\n- Preventing regular v2 write activity (condense, push, agent commit)\n  from running while a migration is in progress. Regular writes must\n  continue to work — see \"Out-of-scope risks\" for separately-tracked\n  hazards.\n- Recovering from corrupted lock state via a CLI flag. OS-level lock\n  release on process death covers all realistic recovery scenarios.\n\n## Decisions\n\n| Decision | Choice | Rationale |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Behavior on contention | Fail fast | One-shot user-initiated command; clearer UX than queueing; avoids hung-process pathologies. |\n| Lock scope | Per git common-dir | Migration mutates shared refs in `.git/`; this is the natural contention domain. Worktree-local would still race; machine-global is overly restrictive. |\n| Lock primitive | OS-level advisory file lock (`flock` on Unix, `LockFileEx` on Windows) via `golang.org/x/sys` | Auto-released by kernel on process death (including SIGKILL). No stale-lock recovery story needed. `golang.org/x/sys` is already a transitive dep. |\n| Coverage | Migration vs migration only | Stated concern. Regular v2 writers continue to operate; their existing CAS guards handle normal-vs-normal contention. Migration-vs-regular hazards exist but are out of scope (see below). |\n\n## Architecture\n\n### `cmd/entire/cli/lockfile` package\n\nNew package isolating the build-tag split.\n\n```\ncmd/entire/cli/lockfile/\n  lockfile.go            // public API (cross-platform)\n  lockfile_unix.go       // //go:build unix\n  lockfile_windows.go    // //go:build windows\n  lockfile_test.go       // unit tests\n```\n\nPublic API:\n\n```go\n// ErrLocked is returned by Acquire when another process holds the lock.\nvar ErrLocked = errors.New(\"lockfile already held\")\n\n// Lock represents an acquired exclusive lock on a path.\ntype Lock struct { /* unexported fields: *os.File + OS-specific state */ }\n\n// Acquire opens path with O_CREATE|O_RDWR|O_CLOEXEC mode 0600, attempts a\n// non-blocking exclusive OS-level lock, and on success writes the current\n// PID into the file (advisory diagnostic only). Returns ErrLocked if\n// another process holds the lock; non-ErrLocked errors indicate I/O or\n// permission failures.\nfunc Acquire(path string) (*Lock, error)\n\n// Release releases the OS lock and closes the underlying file handle.\n// Safe to call exactly once.\nfunc (*Lock) Release() error\n\n// ReadHolderPID returns the PID written into the lock file, or 0 if the\n// file is empty/unreadable/contains garbage. Best-effort; for diagnostic\n// messages only.\nfunc ReadHolderPID(path string) int\n```\n\nOS-specific shims expose a single internal function:\n\n```go\n// tryLockExclusive returns ErrLocked on contention, nil on success,\n// other errors on I/O failures.\nfunc tryLockExclusive(f *os.File) error\nfunc unlock(f *os.File) error\n```\n\nUnix uses `golang.org/x/sys/unix.Flock(fd, LOCK_EX|LOCK_NB)`; `EWOULDBLOCK` →\n`ErrLocked`. Windows uses\n`golang.org/x/sys/windows.LockFileEx(handle, LOCKFILE_EXCLUSIVE_LOCK|LOCKFILE_FAIL_IMMEDIATELY, 0, MAXDWORD, MAXDWORD, &overlapped)`;\n`ERROR_LOCK_VIOLATION` → `ErrLocked`.\n\nThe lock file is **never deleted**. Stale files are harmless because\nthe OS lock state is what counts, and not deleting avoids a\ndelete-then-acquire race on the next run.\n\n### Integration with `entire migrate`\n\nIn `cmd/entire/cli/migrate.go`, the lock wraps the entire migration\nbody (including the post-migration repair pass) but not flag parsing\nor help output:\n\n```go\nRunE: func(cmd *cobra.Command, _ []string) error {\n    if checkpointsFlag == \"\" { return cmd.Help() }\n    if checkpointsFlag != \"v2\" { return fmt.Errorf(...) }\n\n    ctx := cmd.Context()\n    if _, err := paths.WorktreeRoot(ctx); err != nil { /* existing prereq */ }\n\n    commonDir, err := strategy.GetGitCommonDir(ctx)\n    if err != nil { return NewSilentError(err) }\n    lockPath := filepath.Join(commonDir, \"entire-migrate.lock\")\n\n    lk, err := lockfile.Acquire(lockPath)\n    if err != nil {\n        if errors.Is(err, lockfile.ErrLocked) {\n            cmd.SilenceUsage = true\n            holder := lockfile.ReadHolderPID(lockPath)\n            fmt.Fprintf(cmd.ErrOrStderr(),\n                \"another `entire migrate` is already running (PID %d, lock at %s); refusing to start a second instance\\n\",\n                holder, lockPath)\n            return NewSilentError(errors.New(\"migration already in progress\"))\n        }\n        return NewSilentError(fmt.Errorf(\"acquire migration lock: %w\", err))\n    }\n    defer func() {\n        if relErr := lk.Release(); relErr != nil {\n            logging.Warn(ctx, \"failed to release migration lock\", slog.String(\"error\", relErr.Error()))\n        }\n    }()\n\n    // existing logging init + runMigrateCheckpointsV2(...)\n}\n```\n\n`strategy.GetGitCommonDir(ctx)` already exists, is cached, and resolves\ncorrectly for both main worktrees and linked worktrees — the same\ncanonical path is returned for any worktree of the same repo, which is\nexactly the contention domain we want.\n\n`Acquire` runs after flag and prerequisite validation but before\nlogging init, repository open, and any v1/v2 store calls. The failure\nmode for a second invocation is then deterministic: a single line on\nstderr and a non-zero exit before any state is touched.\n\n`Release` is `defer`-ed so we get a chance to log a release error;\ncorrectness does not depend on it because the OS releases on process\ndeath.\n\n## Error handling\n\nThree observable outcomes:\n\n1. **Acquire succeeds.** Migration runs as today. Optional debug log\n   on acquire and