e migrate` against **different**\n41\t  repositories (no value, would just block dev workflows).\n42\t- Preventing regular v2 write activity (condense, push, agent commit)\n43\t  from running while a migration is in progress. Regular writes must\n44\t  continue to work — see \"Out-of-scope risks\" for separately-tracked\n45\t  hazards.\n46\t- Recovering from corrupted lock state via a CLI flag. OS-level lock\n47\t  release on process death covers all realistic recovery scenarios.\n48\t\n49\t## Decisions\n50\t\n51\t| Decision | Choice | Rationale |\n52\t| --- | --- | --- |\n53\t| Behavior on contention | Fail fast | One-shot user-initiated command; clearer UX than queueing; avoids hung-process pathologies. |\n54\t| Lock scope | Per git common-dir | Migration mutates shared refs in `.git/`; this is the natural contention domain. Worktree-local would still race; machine-global is overly restrictive. |\n55\t| Lock primitive | OS-level advisory file lock (`flock` on Unix, `LockFileEx` on Windows) via `golang.org/x/sys` | Auto-released by kernel on process death (including SIGKILL). No stale-lock recovery story needed. `golang.org/x/sys` is already a transitive dep. |\n56\t| Coverage | Migration vs migration only | Stated concern. Regular v2 writers continue to operate; their existing CAS guards handle normal-vs-normal contention. Migration-vs-regular hazards exist but are out of scope (see below). |\n57\t\n58\t## Architecture\n59\t\n60\t### `cmd/entire/cli/lockfile` package"}]},"uuid":"35355104-13ed-4bff-b7a0-1c3bb219f470","timestamp":"2026-05-06T21:03:54.169Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/pfleidi/entire/cli/.worktrees/feat/entire-migrate-lock/docs/superpowers/specs/2026-05-06-entire-migrate-lock-design.md","content":"# `entire migrate` mutual-exclusion lock — design\n\nDate: 2026-05-06\nBranch: `feat/entire-migrate-lock`\n\n## Problem\n\n`entire migrate --checkpoints v2` is a hidden, one-shot, user-initiated\ncommand that orchestrates a long-running, multi-ref pipeline:\n\n- Lists v1 checkpoints, sorts oldest-first.\n- For each v1 checkpoint, reads metadata, builds a compact transcript,\n  writes per-checkpoint v2 `/main/<id>` refs, and queues `/full/*`\n  artifacts.\n- Batches `/full/<n>` archive writes via\n  `writeMigratedFullGeneration` (`cmd/entire/cli/migrate.go:584`).\n- Finalizes with `writeMigratedFinalFullCurrent` and\n  `RotateCurrentGenerationIfNeeded` (`migrate.go:287, 295`).\n- Runs a generation-metadata repair pass over freshly-packed refs.\n\nTwo concurrent `entire migrate` processes against the same repository\ncan corrupt v2 ref state. The most acute risk is the `/full/*` rotation\nmechanism: `NextGenerationNumber()` is read-then-write, so two parallel\nmigrators can both pick the same archive slot, and the final\n`SetReference` call in `writeMigratedFullGeneration` is unconditional —\nthe second writer silently overwrites the first.\n\nThe existing optimistic-concurrency guards in `RotateCurrentGenerationIfNeeded`\n(`cmd/entire/cli/checkpoint/v2_generation.go:530, 583`, using\n`CheckAndSetReference`) are designed for fast condense-vs-condense\ncontention, not for the long, multi-ref migration pipeline.\n\n## Goal\n\nPrevent two `entire migrate` invocations from running concurrently\nagainst the same git repository.\n\n## Non-goals\n\n- Preventing concurrent `entire migrate` against **different**\n  repositories (no value, would just block dev workflows).\n- Preventing regular v2 write activity (condense, push, agent commit)\n  from running while a migration is in progress. Regular writes must\n  continue to work — see \"Out-of-scope risks\" for separately-tracked\n  hazards.\n- Recovering from corrupted lock state via a CLI flag. OS-level lock\n  release on process death covers all realistic recovery scenarios.\n\n## Decisions\n\n| Decision | Choice | Rationale |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Behavior on contention | Fail fast | One-shot user-initiated command; clearer UX than queueing; avoids hung-process pathologies. |\n| Lock scope | Per git common-dir | Migration mutates shared refs in `.git/`; this is the natural contention domain. Worktree-local would still race; machine-global is overly restrictive. |\n| Lock primitive | OS-level advisory file lock (`flock` on Unix, `LockFileEx` on Windows) via `golang.org/x/sys` | Auto-released by kernel on process death (including SIGKILL). No stale-lock recovery story needed. `golang.org/x/sys` is already a transitive dep. |\n| Coverage | Migration vs migration only | Stated concern. Regular v2 writers continue to operate; their existing CAS guards handle normal-vs-normal contention. Migration-vs-regular hazards exist but are out of scope (see below). |\n\n## Architecture\n\n### `cmd/entire/cli/lockfile` package","numLines":60,"startLine":1,"totalLines":368}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e53bdf5d-0c67-472d-9f99-e0be305efdbc","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/pfleidi/entire/cli/.worktrees/feat/entire-migrate-lock","sessionId":"c1af0c92-59d2-43e5-9cec-afe70cbc425d","version":"2.1.119","gitBranch":"feat/entire-migrate-lock"}
